For each ticker that we base our team's investment universe on, the model should give us one of three signals: "long", "put", and "exclude"
Each week is run independently so we replace whatever portfolio we have currently with what the model says we should have each and every week on Friday (because that is how often we can trade)
For example, let's say the model gives a long signal for ADBE and APPL, a put signal for NVDA and META, and a exclude signal for TSLA and AMZN
The model will look at ITM put options prices for NVDA and META that are 1-2 weeks out in expiration, and tell us what is the lowest strike price put option (the least ITM put option) that still has a delta that is reasonably close to 1 (like 0.95 or 9.99) with as little extrinsic value as possible
We will submit orders to buy ADBE and APPL stock and puts on NVDA and META. The model will give us the quantities: it should deploy 100% of the allocated cash and be weighted by the delta adjusted exposure to the underlying notional. This means that exposure should be more than 100% of the underlying (because puts have embedded leverage) but the size of the put position should still be much smaller than the size of the long positions.
There are 2 possible ideas for handling the puts: buying options expiring one week out or 2 weeks out. Either way it should work, because basically every stock we will be trading has weekly expirations.
Idea 1: buy a portfolio of put options that is exactly 1 week out. So on Friday, when we are making those trades, we will simply be replacing expiring options with new 1 week options. This could mean simply buying the next portfolio of options that our model tells us to buy and letting the previous portfolio expire (and automatically extract value from any expiring puts assuming they are still ITM) or actually selling the expiring options and buying new ones.
Idea 2: buy a portfolio of put options that is exactly 2 weeks out. On Friday, when we are making those trades, that portfolio of put options will be exactly 1 week out from expiration. This means that we can just sell that entire portfolio of put options and then buy another fresh set of options with an expiration 2 weeks out based on whatever the model tells us to do. 
The exluded tickers are simply ignored rather than creating a "cash" allocation. That preserves capital efficiency. 
